FRM.registerQuestions(12, [{"id":"315","page":3,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.28"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.14"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.72"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.3"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The probability of an increase in the annual dividend paid out to shareholders of ABC Limited is 0.4. The probability of an increase in share price given an increase in dividends is 0.7. Determine the joint probability of an increase in dividends and an increase in the share price."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Let: A be the event that the dividend is increased and, B be the event that the share price increases Therefore, P(A) = 0.4 and P(B | A) = 0.7 The joint probability of an increase in dividends and an increase in share price is P (B ∩ A)"},{"type":"p","text":"The multiplication rule of probability states that:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (B|A) =  (B ∩ A)\n            P (A)"},{"type":"p","text":"Hence"},{"type":"pre","text":"P(B ∩ A) = P (B|A) ∗ P (A) = 0.7 ∗ 0.4 = 0.28 or 28%"},{"type":"p","text":"(Note that P (A ∩ B) = P (B ∩ A)."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"317","page":4,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.9"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.7"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.4"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A financial risk manager exam candidate is asked two questions. The probability that she gets the first question correct is 0.4 and the probability that she gets the second question correct is 0.5. Given that the probability that she gets both questions correct is 0.2, determine the probability that she gets either the first or the second question correct."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Let A = {gets first question right} and B = {gets second question right} Therefore, P (A) = 0.4, P (B) = 0.5, and P (A ∩ B) = 0.2 We want to determine P (A ∪ B); The addition rule states that P (A ∪ B) = P (A) + P (B)– P (A ∩ B) Hence,"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (A ∪ B) = 0.4 + 0.5– 0.2 = 0.7"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"318","page":5,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.17"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0137"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.03704"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.00137"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An empirical study of ABC stock listed on the New York Exchange reveals that the stock has closed higher on one-third of all days in the past few months. Given that up and down days are independent, determine the probability of ABC stock closing higher for six consecutive days."}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"From the information above, we can establish that the probability of closing higher =                1\n                                                                                                     3\nUsing independence, the  probability of  6  consecutive “highs”  =  1  ∗  1  ∗  1  ∗  1  ∗  1  ∗  1  =   1\n                                                                    3     3     3     3     3     3     729"},{"type":"p","text":"(The calculation above follows from the fact that if A and B are independent events, then P (A ∩ B) = P (A) ∗ P (B).)"}],"remember":["Independence of events in probability theory Probability of independent events occuring consecutively Calculation of probabilities using the multiplication rule for independent events Understanding of basic probability concepts"]},{"id":"319","page":5,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.64"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.3"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.25"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A fruit juice shop allows customers to choose apple juice, mango juice or passion juice. The probability of a customer ordering passion juice is 0.45, mango juice and apple juice 0.19, passion juice and mango juice 0.15, passion juice and apple juice 0.25, passion juice or mango juice 0.6, passion juice or apple juice 0.84, and 0.9 for at least one of them. Find the probability that a customer orders all the three juices."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Let: A be the event that a customer chooses/orders apple juice M be the event that a customer chooses mango juice S be the event that a customer chooses passion fruit We can easily establish that: P (S) = 0.45, P (M ∩ A) = 0.19,P (M ∩ S) = 0.15, P (A ∩ S) = 0.25, P (M ∪ S) = 0.6, P (A ∪ S) = 0.84, P (A ∪ M ∪ S) = 0.9 We need to determine P (A ∩ M ∩ S): Borrowing from the addition rule with three sets, P (A ∪ M ∪ S) = P(A) + P (M) + P(S) − P (M ∩ A) − P(M ∩ S) − P (A ∩ S) + P(A ∩ M ∩ S) … … equation (I)"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (M ∪ S) = P (M) + P (S)– P (M ∩ S),\n      P (M) = 0.6 + 0.15– 0.45 = 0.3"},{"type":"p","text":"Similarly,"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (A ∪ S) = P (A) + P(S) − P (A ∩ S)\n      P (A) = 0.84– 0.45 + 0.25 = 0.64"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore applying equation (I),"},{"type":"pre","text":"0.9 = 0.64 + 0.3 + 0.45– 0.19– 0.15– 0.25 + P (A ∩ M ∩ S)"},{"type":"p","text":"Which gives us P (A ∩ M ∩ S) = 0.1"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"321","page":7,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0125"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.25"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0025"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0625"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"During a lottery, 400 names are fed into a computer program. Five of the names are identical. If a name is drawn from the program at random, what is the probability that one of these 5 names will be drawn?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"P(name 1 ∪ name 2 ∪ name 3 ∪ name 4 ∪ name 5)"},{"type":"pre","text":"1       1       1       1       1"},{"type":"p","text":"= 400 + 400 + 400 + 400 + 400 = 0.0125"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"356","page":8,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.359"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.233"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.216"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.2"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A financial risk manager has three routes to get to the office. The probability that she gets to the office on time using routes X, Y, and Z are 60%, 65%, and 70%. She does not have a preferred route and is therefore equally likely to choose any of the three routes. Calculate the probability that she chose route Z given that she arrives to work on time."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Define X to be the event “chooses route X.” Let Y and Z have similar definitions. Define O to be the event that she arrives on time"},{"type":"p","text":"We wish to determine P (Z|O). Then:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P(Z |O)  =  [P (Z)    ∗  P (O|Z )     P (Z ) ∗ P (O|Z)    +      P (X)  ∗  P (O|X)]\n                                     + P (Y ) ∗ P(O|Y )\n                               (  1  ∗  0.7)\n                                  3\n         =\n                1                 1                 1\n            [(  3  ∗  0.7)  +  (  3  ∗  0.65 )  +(  3  ∗  0.6)]\n         =            0.2333            +  0.2)\n            (0.2333 + 0.2167\n         = 0.3589"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"357","page":9,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.9525"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0593"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.063"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0475"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A life assurance company insures individuals of all ages. A manager compiled the following statistics of the company’s insured persons:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Age of   Mortality                     Portion of company’s\ninsured  (Probability of death)        insured persons\n16-20    [arbitrary]                               0.10\n21-30                 0.04                         0.29\n31-65                 0.05                         0.49\n66-99                 0.10                         0.12\n                      0.14"},{"type":"p","text":"If a randomly selected individual insured by the company dies, calculate the probability that the dead client was age 16-20."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Define the following events: B = Event of death B1= Event the insured’s age is in the range 16-20 B2= Event the insured’s age is in the range 21-30 B3= Event the insured’s age is in the range 31-65 B4= Event the insured’s age is in the range 66-99 We wish to determine P (B1|B)"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (B1|B) =  [P (B1)                               (P (B1) ∗ P (B|B1))\n                     ∗ P(B|B1) + (P (B2) ∗ P (B|B2) + (P (B3) ∗ P (B|B3) + (P(B4) ∗ P (B|B4)]\n                     (0.1 ∗ 0.04)"},{"type":"p","text":"= [(0.1 ∗ 0.04) + (0.29 ∗ 0.05) + (0.49 ∗ 0.1) + (0.12 ∗ 0.14)"},{"type":"pre","text":"=                             0.004\n            (0.004 + 0.0145 + 0.049 + 0.0168)]"},{"type":"p","text":"= 0.04745 or 4.7%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"358","page":10,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.172"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.04"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.168"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.145"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A life assurance company insures individuals of all ages. A manager compiled the following statistics of the company’s insured persons:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Age of             Mortality               Portion of company’s\ninsured            (Probability of death)  insured persons\n16 − 20            [arbitrary]                         0.10\n21 − 30                         0.04                   0.29\n31 − 65                         0.05                   0.49\n66 − 99                         0.10                   0.12\n                                0.14"},{"type":"p","text":"If a randomly selected individual insured by the company dies, calculate the probability that the dead client was in age range 21-30."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We wish to determine P (B2|B)"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (B2|B) =  [P (B2)                               (P (B2) ∗ P (B|B2))\n                     ∗ P(B|B2) + (P (B1) ∗ P (B|B1) + (P (B3) ∗ P (B|B3) + (P(B4) ∗ P (B|B4)]\n                               (0.29 ∗ 0.05)"},{"type":"p","text":"= [(0.29 ∗ 0.05) + (0.1 ∗ 0.04) + (0.49 ∗ 0.1) + (0.12 ∗ 0.14)"},{"type":"pre","text":"=           (0.0145      0.0145\n                     + 0.004 + 0.049 + 0.0168)"},{"type":"p","text":"= 17.2%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"360","page":11,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.047"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.199"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.12"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.201"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A life assurance company insures individuals of all ages. A manager compiled the following statistics of the company’s insured persons:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Age of   Mortality               Portion of company’s\ninsured  (Probability of death)  insured persons\n16-20    [arbitrary]                         0.10\n21-30                 0.04                   0.29\n31-65                 0.05                   0.49\n66-99                 0.10                   0.12\n                      0.14"},{"type":"p","text":"Calculate the probability that the dead client was between 66 and 99 years."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"To calculate the probability that the dead client was between 66 and 99 years, we need to use Bayes' theorem, which states:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (A|B) =  P (B|A) × P (A)\n                 P (B)"},{"type":"p","text":"In this case, we want to calculate the probability that the dead client was between 66 and 99 years old (event A) given that they have died (event B). We know the mortality rates and the proportion of insured persons for each age group, so we can calculate the probabilities of dying and the marginal probability of dying:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P(Dying) = 0.04 × 0.10 + 0.05 × 0.29 + 0.10 × 0.49 + 0.14 × 0.12 = 0.0843"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore,"},{"type":"pre","text":"P(66-99|Dying) =  P(Dying|66-99) × P(66-99)\n                                 P(Dying)\n         = 0.01.408×403.12= 0.1993"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"361","page":12,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.79"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.73"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.22"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.15"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An investment firm classifies capital projects into three different categories, depending on risk level: Standard, Preferred, and Ultra-preferred. Of the firm’s projects, 60% are standard, 30% are preferred, and 10% are ultra-preferred. The probabilities of a project making a loss are 0.01, 0.005, and 0.001 for categories standard, preferred, and ultra-preferred respectively. If a capital project makes a loss in the next year, then what is the probability that the project was standard (correct to 2 decimal places)?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Let: L = Event a project makes a loss S = Event of a standard project P1 = Event of a preferred project U = Event of a ultra-preferred project We wish to determine P (S|L)"},{"type":"pre","text":"(P (S) ∗ P (L|S))"},{"type":"p","text":"P (S |L) = [P (P1) ∗ P (L|P1) + P (U) ∗ P (L|U)]"},{"type":"pre","text":"(0.6 ∗ 0.01)"},{"type":"p","text":"= [(0.6 ∗ 0.01) + (0.3 ∗ 0.005) + (0.1 ∗ 0.001)]"},{"type":"pre","text":"=              0.006"},{"type":"p","text":"[0.006 + 0.0015 + 0.0001]"},{"type":"p","text":"= 0.7895 or 79%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"363","page":13,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.86"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.36"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.5"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Upon arrival at a cancer treatment center, patients are categorized into one of four stages, namely: stage 1, stage 2, stage 3, and stage 4. In the past year, i. 10% of patients arriving were in stage 1 ii. 40% of patients arriving were in stage 2 iii. 30% of patients arriving were in stage 3 iv. The rest of the patients were in stage 4 v. 10% of stage 1 patients died vi. 20% of stage 2 patients died vii. 30% of stage 3 patients died viii. 50% of stage 4 patient died"},{"type":"p","text":"Given that the patient died, what is the probability that the patient was in stage 4 cancer?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We wish to determine P (C4|D)"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (C4|D) =  [(P (C4) ∗ P (D|C4)               (P (C4) ∗ P(D|C4))\n                                 + ((P (C1) ∗ P (D|C1) + (P(C2) ∗ P (D|C2) + (P (C3) ∗ P(D|C3)]\n                            (0.2 ∗ 0.5)"},{"type":"p","text":"= [(0.2 ∗ 0.5) + (0.1 ∗ 0.1) + (0.4 ∗ 0.2) + (0.3 ∗ 0.3)]"},{"type":"pre","text":"=           (0.1         0.1\n                  + 0.01 + 0.08 + 0.09)"},{"type":"p","text":"= 36%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"364","page":13,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.3433"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.15988"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.6567"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.16"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"You are an analyst at a large mutual fund. After examining historical data, you establish that all fund managers fall into two categories: superstars (S) and ordinaries (O). Superstars are by far the best managers. The probability that a superstar will beat the market in any given year stands at 70%. Ordinaries, on the other hand, are just as likely to beat the market as they are to underperform it. Regardless of the category in which a manager falls, the probability of beating the market is independent from year to year. Superstars are rare diamonds because only a meager 16% of all recruits turn out to be superstars."},{"type":"p","text":"During the analysis, you stumble upon the profile of a manager recruited three years ago, who has since gone on to beat the market every year."},{"type":"p","text":"Determine the probability that the manager is a superstar today, given that he has beaten the market for three consecutive years."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Let's define the following events:"},{"type":"pre","text":"S: the manager is a superstar"},{"type":"p","text":"O: the manager is ordinary"},{"type":"p","text":"B: the manager beats the market in a given year"},{"type":"pre","text":"U: the manager underperforms the market in a given year"},{"type":"p","text":"From the information given in the problem, we have:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (S) = 0.16 (the prior probability of being a superstar)"},{"type":"p","text":"P (O) = 0.84 (the prior probability of being ordinary)"},{"type":"p","text":"P (B|S ) = 0.7 (the probability of beating the market given the manager is a superstar)"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (B|O) = 0.5 (the probability of beating the market given the manager is ordinary)"},{"type":"p","text":"We want to find P (S |B3), the probability that the manager is a superstar today, given that he has beaten the market for three consecutive years."},{"type":"p","text":"Using Bayes' theorem, we have:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (S|B3) =       P(B3|S) ⋅ P (S)\n                      P (B3)"},{"type":"p","text":"We can calculate each of the terms separately first:"},{"type":"p","text":"P (B3|S ) = P (B|S)3 = 0.73 = 0.343 (the probability of beating the market for three consecutive years given the manager is a superstar)"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (B3) = P(B3|S) ⋅ P (S ) + P (B3|O) ⋅ P(O) = 0.343 ⋅ 0.16 + 0.53 ⋅ 0.84 = 0.15988"},{"type":"p","text":"(the probability of beating the market for three consecutive years regardless of whether the manager is a superstar or ordinary)"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (S|B3) = 00.3.14539⋅808.16≈ 0.3433"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore, the probability that the manager is a superstar today, given that he has beaten the market for three consecutive years, is approximately 0.3433."}],"remember":["Bayes' Theorem is a fundamental concept in probability theory that describes the probability of an event, based on prior knowledge of conditions that might be related to the event.","Conditional probability is the probability of an event given that another event has already occurred.","Independence of events means that the occurrence of one event does not affect the probability of the occurrence of another event.","Prior probability is the initial probability of an event before new evidence is taken into account.","Posterior probability is the revised probability of an event after taking into account new evidence."]},{"id":"365","page":15,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.46"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.34"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.84"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.16"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"You are an analyst at a large mutual fund. After examining historical data, you establish that all fund managers fall into 2 categories: superstars (S) and ordinaries (O). Superstars are by far the best managers. The probability that a superstar will beat the market in any given year stands at 70%. Ordinaries, on the other hand, are just as likely to beat the market as they are to underperform it. Regardless of the category in which a manager falls, the probability of beating the market is independent of year to year. Superstars are rare diamonds because only a meager 16% of all recruits turn out to be superstars."},{"type":"p","text":"During the analysis, you stumble upon the profile of a manager recruited 3 years ago, who has since gone on to beat the market every year."},{"type":"p","text":"What is the probability that the manager is a superstar as at present?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We need to determine P(S | 3B): The probability that the manager is a superstar given that they have managed to beat the market in three consecutive years. As such, we need to apply Bayes' theorem."},{"type":"pre","text":"                                                       P (S|3B)  =  P (S)  ∗  P (3B|S)\n                                                                               P (3B)\nNow,  we  already       have     P (S)    =  16%       =  4\n                                                          25\n              P (3B|S )  =    (  7    3  since   performance        is  independent     from  one  year  to  the  next\n                                 10  )\n                         =    343\n                              1000"},{"type":"p","text":"P (3B) = unconditional probability of beating the market in 3 consecutive years"},{"type":"pre","text":"   = weighted average probability of 3 marketing-beating years over both superstars and ordinaries\n   = P(3B|S) ∗ P(S) + P (3B|O) ∗ P (O)\n=   [(  170)3  ∗  245]  +  [(  1    3  ∗  21  ]\n                               2          25\n                                   )\n=   ( 1304030∗    245)  +(    1  ∗  2251)\n                              8\n    215307020+    21\n=                 200\n= 16%"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore,"},{"type":"pre","text":"16%   ∗   34.3%     34.3%  or  0.343\n          16% ="}],"remember":[]},{"id":"366","page":17,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.66"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.7"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.45"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.64"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"You are an analyst at a large mutual fund. After examining historical data, you establish that all fund managers fall into two categories: superstars (S) and ordinaries (O). Superstars are by far the best managers. The probability that a superstar will beat the market in any given year stands at 70%. Ordinaries, on the other hand, are just as likely to beat the market as they are to underperform it. Regardless of the category in which a manager falls, the probability of beating the market is independent of year to year. Superstars are rare diamonds because only a meager 16% of all recruits turn out to be superstars."},{"type":"p","text":"During the analysis, you stumble upon the profile of a manager recruited three years ago, who has since gone on to beat the market every year."},{"type":"p","text":"What is the conditional probability for a non-superstar manager given that he/she has beaten the market for 3 years?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"First, we need to determine P(S | 3B): The probability that the manager is a superstar given that they have managed to beat the market in three consecutive years. As such, we need to apply Bayes' theorem."},{"type":"pre","text":"                                           P (S|3B)  =  P (S)  ∗  P (3B|S)\n                                                                   P (3B)\nNow,  we  already  have    P (S)   =  16%  =  4\n                                              25\n          P (3B|S )  =  (  7    3  since  performance   is  independent     from  one  year  to  the  next\n                           10  )\n                     =  343"},{"type":"p","text":"P (3B) = unconditional probability of beating the market in 3 consecutive years"},{"type":"pre","text":"         = weighted average probability of 3 marketing-beating years over both superstars and ordinaries\n         = P(3B|S) ∗ P(S) + P (3B|O) ∗ P (O)\n=  [(       170)3  ∗  245]  +  [(  1   3  ∗  21  ]\n                                   2         25\n                                      )\n=  ( 1304030∗         245)  +(  1  ∗  2251)\n                                8\n   215307020+         21\n=                     200"},{"type":"p","text":"= 16%"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore,"},{"type":"pre","text":"16% ∗ 3146.3%%= 0.343"},{"type":"p","text":"Finally, P(O|3B) = 1– P (S|3B) = 1– 0.343 = 0.66"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"367","page":18,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"19%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"53%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"47%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"18%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A human health organization tracked a group of individuals for five years. At the commencement of the study, 25% were categorized as heavy smokers, 40% as light smokers, and the remaining as nonsmokers. Results revealed that light smokers were twice as likely as nonsmokers to die during the half-decade study, but only half as likely as heavy smokers. During the period, a randomly selected group member passed on. Compute the probability that the individual who died was a heavy smoker."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"To solve this problem, we use Bayes' Theorem to find the probability that an individual who died was a heavy smoker, given the probabilities of dying for each group of individuals. Define:"},{"type":"pre","text":"H : The event that an individual is a heavy smoker.\nL: The event that an individual is a light smoker.\nN : The event that an individual is a nonsmoker.\nD : The event that an individual dies."},{"type":"p","text":"Given probabilities:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (H) = 0.25 (25% are heavy smokers)\n P (L) = 0.40 (40% are light smokers)\nP (N) = 0.35 (remaining 35% are nonsmokers)"},{"type":"p","text":"Let P (D|N) = x be the probability that a nonsmoker dies. Thus:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Probability that light smokers are twice as likely to die compared to nonsmokers:\nP (D|L) = 2x\nProbability that heavy smokers are twice as likely as light smokers to die, hence four\ntimes as likely as nonsmokers: P(D|H) = 4x"},{"type":"p","text":"Using the law of total probability for P (D):"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (D) = P (D|H )P(H ) + P (D|L)P (L) + P (D|N )P (N )\n       = 4x ⋅ 0.25 + 2x ⋅ 0.40 + x ⋅ 0.35\n       = 1.0x + 0.8x + 0.35x\n       = 2.15x"},{"type":"p","text":"Calculate P (H|D):"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (H |D)  =  P (D|H)P (H)  =  42x.1⋅ 50.x25=  1.0   ≈  0.4651\n                  P (D )                      2.15"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore, the probability that the individual who died was a heavy smoker is approximately 47% ."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"369","page":20,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"1 3"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"2 3"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.5"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.75"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Peter selects a coin from a pair of coins and tosses it. While coin 1 is double-headed, coin 2 is a normal unbiased coin. After the toss, the result is a head. Calculate the probability that it was coin 1 which was tossed."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We need to determine P (coin 1|head). By applying Bayes’ theorem,"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (coin 1|head) =                     P (coin 1) × P(head|coin 1)                           (1     ×  1  )        2\n                                1) × P (head|coin 1) + P (coin 2)                                  +  2           3\n                                                                            (head|co=in 12 ×))                 =\n                   (P(coin                                            ×  P                      1     1  ×  1\n                                                                                                2     2     2"},{"type":"p","text":"Note:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (coin 1) = P (coin 2) =  1    since  we    have  two  coins  (pair)  of  coins  that   are      equally  likely\n                           2\nto occur\nP (head |coin 1) = 1 since coin 1 is double headed\nP (head  |coin  2) =  1  since  coin  2  is  unbiased   since  both  the  head  and  the    tail     are  equally\n                      2\nlikely to occur"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3251","page":21,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"14%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"32%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"44%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"6%"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The probability that the Eurozone economy will grow this year is 18%, and the probability that the European Central Bank (ECB) will loosen its monetary policy is 26%. Assuming that the joint probability that the Eurozone economy will grow and the ECB will loosen its monetary policy is 12%, then the probability that either the Eurozone economy will grow or the ECB will loosen its the monetary policy is closest to:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The addition rule of probability is used to solve this question: P (E) = 0.18 (the probability that the Eurozone economy will grow is 18%) p(M) = 0.26 (the probability that the ECB will loosen the monetary policy is 26%) p(EM) = 0.12 (the joint probability that Eurozone economy will grow and the ECB will loosen its monetary policy is 12%) The probability that either the Eurozone economy will grow or the central bank will loosen its the monetary policy:"},{"type":"pre","text":"p(E or M) = p(E) + p(M) − p(EM) = 0.18 + 0.26 − 0.12 = 0.32"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3252","page":22,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.5675"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.4325"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.3265"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.3333"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A mathematician has given you the following conditional probabilities:"},{"type":"pre","text":"p(O|T) = 0.62          Conditional probability of reaching the office if the train arrives on time"},{"type":"p","text":"p(O|T c) = 0.47 Conditional probability of reaching the office if the train does not arrive on time"},{"type":"pre","text":"p(T ) = 0.65                       Unconditional probability of the train arriving on time\n   p(O) =?                              Unconditional probability of reaching the office"},{"type":"p","text":"What is the unconditional probability of reaching the office, p(O)?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"This question can be solved using the total probability rule."},{"type":"p","text":"If p(T ) = 0.65 (Unconditional probability of train arriving at time is 0.65), then the unconditional probability of the train not arriving on time p(T c) = 1 − p(T ) = 1 − 0.65 = 0.35."},{"type":"p","text":"Now, we can solve for p(O) = p(O|T ) ∗ p(T ) + p(O|T c) ∗ p(Tc) = 0.62 ∗ 0.65 + 0.47 ∗ 0.35 = 0.5675"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3254","page":23,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.58"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.12"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.7"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.67"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An investor owns shares of both Apple and Microsoft. The two companies operate independently. The investor assumes that the probability of Apple's share price declining by more than 5% this year is 0.4 while the probability of Microsoft's share price declining by more than 5% is 0.3. What is the probability that either Apple or Microsoft share prices will decline in price by more than 5% this year?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We know that,"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (A ∪ B) = P (A) + P(B) − P (A ∩ B)\n             = P (A) + P(B) − P (A) ∗ P (B)\n             = 0.4 + 0.3 − 0.4 ∗ 0.3 = 0.58"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3255","page":24,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"62.50%."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"50%."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"80%."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"37.50%"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The probabilities that Bond A and Bond X will default in the next two years are 10% and 8%, respectively. The probability that both bonds will default simultaneously in the next two years is 5%. The probability that Bond A will default given that Bond X has already defaulted is closest to:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"P (X) = 8% P (A) = 10% P (X ∩ A) = 5% As per the conditional probability:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (A|X) =                            P (X ∩ A)  =  5%  = 62.5%\n                                        P (X)      8%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3256","page":24,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.72"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.67"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.85"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.61"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"There is a 40% chance that ABX will announce negative quarterly results tomorrow. On any given day, there is a 55% chance that the company's stock price will decrease. If negative quarterly results are announced, the probability that the stock price will decline is 85%. Tomorrow, the probability that ABX will announce negative quarterly results or that the stock will decrease in price is closest to:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"To calculate the probability that ABX will announce negative quarterly results or that the stock will decrease in price, we can use the addition rule of probability. The probability of event A occurring is given as 0.40, and the probability of event B occurring is given as 0.55. The probability of event B given event A (stock price decreasing given negative quarterly results) is given as 0.85. Using the addition rule, the probability of either event A or event B occurring is calculated as: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B) Since the events are independent, P(A and B) = P(A) * P(B|A) Substituting the given probabilities: P(A or B) = 0.40 + 0.55 - (0.40 * 0.85) P(A or B) = 0.95 - 0.34 P(A or B) ≈ 0.61"}],"remember":["The addition rule of probability states that the probability of either of two mutually exclusive events occurring is the sum of their individual probabilities.","The multiplication rule of probability is used when events are not independent, and it calculates the probability of both events occurring.","Independence of events means that the occurrence of one event does not affect the probability of the other event occurring.","Conditional probability is the probability of one event happening given that another event has already occurred.","Bayes' Theorem is a formula that describes how to update the probabilities of hypotheses when given evidence."]},{"id":"3257","page":26,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.30."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.40."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.50."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.6"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The probability that a portfolio manager reads Business News weekly is 0.50, while the probability that a portfolio manager reads BloomField News is 0.40. If the probability that a portfolio manager reads both Business News and BloomField News is 0.30, then the probability that a portfolio manager does not read any of the two newspapers is closest to:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"For simplicity, let A = Business News; and B = BloomField News. p(A) = 0.50 p(B) = 0.40 P (A ∩ B) = 0.30"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (A ∪ B) = P (A) + P(B) − P (A ∩ B)\nP (A ∪ B) = 0.50 + 0.40 − 0.30 = 0.60"},{"type":"p","text":"The probability that someone reads A or B is 0.60. Therefore, the probability that a person does not read any of the two:"},{"type":"pre","text":"p(person does not read any of the two) = 1 − 0.60 = 0.40"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3590","page":26,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.2"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.5"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.6"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An athlete takes part in two different events. The probability that she wins the first event is 0.3 and the probability that she wins the second event is 0.4. Given that the probability that she wins the first and the second event is 0.1, calculate the probability that she wins either the first or the second event."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"If A = P(wins first event) and B = P(wins second event), then we are told: P(A) = 0.3; P(B) = 0.4; and P (A ∩ B) = 0.1 We want,"},{"type":"pre","text":"                                                                    P(A ∪ B)\nRecall that:              P (A ∪ B) = P (A) + P (B) − P(A ∩ B)\nThus,                      P (A ∪ B) = 0.3 + 0.4 − 0.1 = 0.6"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3591","page":28,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"15%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"25%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"30%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"45%"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A homeowners insurer offers a discount for homeowners that either have a sprinkler system or live within 5 miles of a fire station. 60% of homeowners qualify for the discount. Only 15% of homeowners have a sprinkler system, and none of those homeowners live within 5 miles of a fire station. If the events are mutually exclusive, what is the probability a randomly selected homeowner lives within 5 miles of a fire station?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We use the formula:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (A ∪ B) = P (A) + P (B) − P (A ∩ B)"},{"type":"p","text":"However, since the events are mutually exclusive, then P (A ∩ B) = 0, Thus,"},{"type":"pre","text":".60 = .15 + x − 0\n X = .45 = 45%"}],"remember":["Two events, A and B, are said to be mutually exclusive if the occurrence of A rules out the occurrence of B, and vice versa. For example, a car cannot turn left and turn right at the same time.","For two mutually exclusive events, A and B, there's no intersection, and therefore P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) For non-mutually exclusive events, however, the intersection contains some outcomes and has to be subtracted to get P(A or B)"]},{"id":"3592","page":29,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"45%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"50%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"55%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"60%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A patient is considered high risk for a heart attack if they either have high cholesterol or high blood pressure. What percentage of patients are NOT considered high risk for a heart attack if 25% have high cholesterol, 30% have high blood pressure and 10% have both high cholesterol and high blood pressure?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"P (A ∪ B) = P (A) + P (B) − P (A ∩ B)\n           .25 + .30 − .10 = .45"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore, 45% of patients are considered high risk. We now need to find the percentage of patients NOT considered high risk:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P(A ∪ B)′ = 1 − P (A ∪ B) = 1 − .45 = .55 or 55%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3596","page":30,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"83%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"90%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"91%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"93%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"55% of an insurer's policyholders are male and 45% are female. The chances of a male having a claim stand at 10% while the chances of a female having a claim stand at 7%. What is the probability that NO ONE will have a claim?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We know that: P(Male) = 55% P(Female) = 45% P(Claim|Male)=P(Claim given it's a Male)=10% P(Claim|Female)=P(Claim given it's a Female)=7%"},{"type":"p","text":"This question makes use of the conditional probability rule:"},{"type":"p","text":"P (Claim|Male) = P (Claim ∩ Male)"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (Male)"},{"type":"p","text":"⇒ P (Claim ∩ Male) = P (Claim|Male) × P(Male) = .55 × .10"},{"type":"p","text":"We now have to also add together male and female drivers:"},{"type":"pre","text":".55 × .10 + .45 × .07 = .0865"},{"type":"p","text":"And since we're interested in no one having a claim:"},{"type":"pre","text":"1 − .0865 = .9135 or 91%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3601","page":31,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"73.5%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"76.5%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"77.5%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"87.5%"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A company insures both male and female drivers. At the moment, the company has insured an equal number of male and female drivers. Males have a 0.15 chance of having a claim during a policy period while females have a 0.10 chance of having a claim. If a driver is randomly selected from the population, what is the probability that the driver has no claim during the policy period?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We know that: P(Male) = 50% P(Female) =50% P(Claim|Male)=P(Claim given it's a Male)=0.15 P(Claim|Female)=P(Claim given it's a Female)=0.10"},{"type":"p","text":"This question makes use of the conditional probability rule:"},{"type":"p","text":"P (Claim|Male) = P (Claim ∩ Male)"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (Male)"},{"type":"p","text":"⇒ P (Claim ∩ Male) = P (Claim|Male) × P (Male) = 0.15 × 0.50"},{"type":"p","text":"⇒ P (Claim ∩ Female) = P (Claim|Female) × P (Female) = 0.10 × 0.50"},{"type":"p","text":"We now have to also add together male and female drivers:"},{"type":"pre","text":"0.15 × 0.50 + 0.10 × 0.50 = 0.125"},{"type":"p","text":"And since we're interested in the probability that the selected driver has no claim:"},{"type":"pre","text":"1 − 0.125 = 0.875 or 87.5%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3603","page":32,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.15"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.25"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.35"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.6"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"For a certain insured, the probability of making no claim during a policy period is 0.60. The probability of making only 1 claim is 0.25. What is the probability that this insured makes more than 1 claim during the policy period?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"“More than 1” is the complement of “no more than 1” which is 0 or 1"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (A ∪ B) = P (A) + P (B) = .60 + .25 = .85\n P (A ∪ B)′ = 1 − P (A ∪ B) = 1 − .85 = .15"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3606","page":33,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"35%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"65%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"57%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"43%"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"60% of an insurer's policyholders are male and 40% are female. The chance of a female having a claim is twice the chance of a male having a claim. Given a randomly selected policyholder has a claim, what's the probability that the policyholder is a male?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Let the probability of a male having a claim be P(C|M) and that of a female having a claim be P(C|F)."},{"type":"p","text":"The chance of a female having a claim is twice the chance of a male having a claim, that is, 2P(C|M) = P(C|F)"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus,"},{"type":"pre","text":"[P (C|M)* P(M)]"},{"type":"p","text":"P(M|C) = [P(M)*P(C|M)+P(F)*P(C|F)"},{"type":"pre","text":"            [P (C|M) ∗ 0.60)]\n= [0.60 ∗ P(C|M) + 0.40 ∗ P(C|F)"},{"type":"p","text":"Replacing 2P(C|M) = P (C|F)"},{"type":"pre","text":"[P (C|M) ∗ 0.60)]"},{"type":"p","text":"P(M|C) = [0.60 ∗ P(C|M) + 0.40 ∗ 2 ∗ P(C|M)"},{"type":"pre","text":"=       0.60"},{"type":"p","text":"(0.60 + 0.80)"},{"type":"p","text":"= 0.4286 = 42.86%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3607","page":34,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"25%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"33%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"36%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"38%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"55% of an insurer's policyholders are male and 45% are female. The chance of a male having a claim is 10% and the chance of a female having a claim is 7%. Given a randomly selected policyholder has a claim, what's the probability she is a female?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"This question makes use of Bayes' theorem. We know that: P (M) = 0.55 and P (F) = 0.45 Also, P (C|M) = 0.10 and P (C|F ) = 0.07 Now, we need P (F |C). Using Bayes' Theorem,"},{"type":"pre","text":"P(F|C) =             P (C|F ). P (F )\n          P(C|F ).P (F ) + P (C |M). P(M)\n                 0.07 × 0.45\n=         0.07 × 0.45 + 0.10 × 0.55\n=         0.0315\n          0.0865"},{"type":"p","text":"= 0.3642 ≈ 36%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3609","page":35,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"20%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"25%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"33%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"40%"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A company insures red and black cars, male and female drivers and writes policies in 2 territories (A and B). There are 300 male drivers and 200 female drivers in total. There are 150 males who drive red cars and 100 females who drive red cars. 100 male and 100 female drivers live in territory A and 50 of each, males and females, drive red cars in territory A. Given that a randomly selected policyholder drives a black car, what is the probability that they are female and live in territory B?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":" M ale   Red Car   Territory A                      Territory B\n M ale  Black Car         50                              100\nFemale   Red Car          50                              100\nFemale  Black Car         50                               50\n                          50                               50"},{"type":"p","text":"P ( Female and Territory B | Black Car ) = P (Female, Territory B and Black Car)"},{"type":"pre","text":"                                 P (Black Car)\n=                        50   =  .20 or 20%\n                         250"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3610","page":35,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"15%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"20%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"25%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"33%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A patient is considered high risk for a heart attack if they either have high cholesterol or high blood pressure and the two events are independent. In a given population, 25% have high cholesterol and 30% have high blood pressure. If a randomly selected person has high blood pressure, what is the probability they also have high cholesterol?z"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Let's denote the following probabilities:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P(C) = Probability of having high cholesterol = 0.25\nP(B) = Probability of having high blood pressure = 0.30\nP(C ∩ B) = Probability of having both high cholesterol and high blood pressure"},{"type":"p","text":"Since the events are independent: P(C ∩ B) = P(C) × P(B) Now, substitute the given probabilities: P(C ∩ B) = 0.25 × 0.30 = 0.075 Next, we need to find the conditional probability that a person has high cholesterol given that they have high blood pressure, denoted as P(C|B). Using the definition of conditional probability: P(C|B) = P(C ∩ B) / P(B) Substitute the known values: P(C|B) = 0.075 / 0.30 = 0.25 Therefore, the probability that a person has high cholesterol given that they have high blood pressure is 0.25."}],"remember":["Conditional Probability: Conditional probability is the probability of an event occurring given that another event has already occurred. It is denoted as P(A|B) where A and B are events.","Independence of Events: Two events are considered independent if the occurrence of one event does not affect the occurrence of the other event. Mathematically, P(A ∩ B) = P(A) * P(B) if A and B are independent events.","Marginal Probability: Marginal probability is the probability of a single event occurring without any reference to any other event. It is denoted as P(A) or P(B).","Joint Probability: Joint probability is the probability of two events occurring together. It is denoted as P(A ∩ B).","Bayes' Theorem: Bayes' Theorem is a mathematical formula used to determine the conditional probability of an event based on prior knowledge of conditions that might be related to the event."]},{"id":"3611","page":37,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"15%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"20%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"25%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"33%"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A patient is considered high risk for a heart attack if they either have high cholesterol or high blood pressure. In a given population, 45% of people are considered high risk for a heart attack, (25% have high cholesterol, 30% have high blood pressure). If a randomly selected person has high blood pressure, what is the probability they also have high cholesterol?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"P (Both)"},{"type":"p","text":"P (High Cholesterol ∣ High Blood Pressure) = P (High BP)"},{"type":"p","text":"P (A ∪ B) = P (A) + P (B) − P (A ∩ B)"},{"type":"p","text":".45 = .25 + .30 − x"},{"type":"p","text":"P (A ∩ B) = .10"},{"type":"pre","text":"  P (Both)     .10  =  1  or  33%\nP (High BP) =  .30     3"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3613","page":38,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"8%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"10%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"20%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"23%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Given the following chart describing the claims of an auto insurer during a policy period, calculate P (C|M). (Assume that the number of male and female claims are independent of each other.)"},{"type":"pre","text":"Claim (C)     Male (M)  Female (F)       Total\nNo Claim (X)     100         200         300\nTotal            400         600         1000\n                 500         800         1300"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"To find P (C ∣ M), the probability of a claim given that the policyholder is male, we use the formula for conditional probability:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (C          ∣  M)  =  P (C ∩ M)\n                           P (M)"},{"type":"p","text":"Where:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (C ∩ M) is the joint probability of both a claim and the policyholder being male.\nP (M) is the marginal probability of the policyholder being male."},{"type":"p","text":"From the table, we can find these probabilities:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (C ∩ M) = 1103000, as there are 100 claims by males out of 1300 total observations.\nP (M) = 1530000, as there are 500 males out of 1300 total observations."},{"type":"p","text":"Now we can calculate the conditional probability:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (C ∣ M) =  P(C ∩ M)   100   =  100  =  20%\n               P (M) =  1300     500\n                        500\n                        1300"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore, the probability that a claim is made given that the policyholder is male is 20%."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3615","page":39,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"25%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"30%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"33%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"45%"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An insurance company classifies its policyholders into three tiers – standard, preferred, and ultra preferred. 40% of standard tier policyholders are male, 50% of preferred tier policyholders are male and 75% of ultra preferred tier policyholders are male. There is an equal number of policyholders in each tier. If a policyholder is selected at random, what is the chance she is female?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Let event F be “female”, event S be “standard”, event P be “preferred” and event U be “ultrapreferred” P (F ) = P (F /S) ∗ P (S ) + P (F /P ) ∗ P (P ) + P (F /U ) ∗ P (U) P (F ) = .60 ∗ (1/3) + .50 ∗ (1/3) + .25 ∗ (1/3) P (F ) = .45 or 45%"}],"remember":["Probability theory is a branch of mathematics that deals with calculating the likelihood of a given event's occurrence.","Conditional probability is the probability of an event occurring given that another event has already occurred.","Bayes' Theorem is a fundamental theorem in probability theory that describes the probability of an event based on prior knowledge of conditions that might be related to the event.","Gender distribution in a population is an important factor to consider when calculating probabilities related to gender-specific events."]},{"id":"3616","page":40,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"15%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"24%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"30%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"33%"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An insurance company classifies its policyholders into three tiers – standard, preferred, and ultra preferred. 40% of standard tier policyholders are male, 50% of preferred tier policyholders are male and 75% of ultra preferred tier policyholders are male. There is an equal number of policyholders in each tier. If a male policyholder is selected at random, what is the chance he is classified as a standard tier?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Using Bayes Theorem,"},{"type":"pre","text":"P     (S/M )  =  P   (M/S) × P      (S)\n                         P (M)"},{"type":"p","text":"Where"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (M) = P (M/S) × P (S ) + P (M/P ) × P (P ) + P (M/U) × P (U )\n               =  0.40  ×  (  1  )  +  0.50  ×  (  1  )  +  0.75  ×  (  1  )\n                              3                    3                    3\n               = 0.55"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus,"},{"type":"pre","text":"                (0.40 × (1/3))\nP (S/M) = (0.55) = 0.24 or 24%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3617","page":41,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"5%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"7%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"9%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"11%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An insurance company classifies its policyholders into three tiers – standard, preferred and ultra preferred with a 25%/50%/25% distribution. The chance of a policyholder in the standard tier having a claim is 10%, in the preferred tier it is 5% and in the ultra preferred tier it is 2%. Given a policyholder has a claim, what is the probability they came from the ultra preferred tier?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"To find this probability, we used Bayes' Theorem:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P(U |C)  =  P (C|U)P (U)\n                 P (C)"},{"type":"p","text":"Where:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (C) = P (C|S)P (S) + P(C|P )P (P ) + P(C|U)P (U)"},{"type":"p","text":"Given the information:"},{"type":"pre","text":"The distribution of policyholders is 25% standard, 50% preferred, and 25% ultra\npreferred.\nThe probability of a claim for a policyholder in the standard tier is 10%, in the\npreferred tier is 5%, and in the ultra preferred tier is 2%."},{"type":"p","text":"We calculated the overall probability of a claim, P(C), as:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (C) = 0.10 × 0.25 + 0.05 × 0.50 + 0.02 × 0.25 = 0.055"},{"type":"p","text":"Finally, we calculated the probability that a policyholder came from the ultra preferred tier given that they have a claim:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (U|C) = 0.002.0×550.25= 0.09 = 9%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3618","page":42,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"1/5"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"1/3"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"2/5"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"2/3"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"There are three different bags. The first bag contains 3 square blocks and 2 round blocks. The second bag contains 2 square blocks and 3 round blocks. The third bag contains 5 round blocks. In an experiment, a bag is randomly chosen, and then a block is chosen from the bag. What is the probability that a round block is chosen?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Let:"},{"type":"p","text":"P (R)=Probability that the block is round"},{"type":"p","text":"P (R/1)= Probability that the block is round given that it is from the first bag = 2"},{"type":"pre","text":"                                                                                                    5\nP (R/2)=   Probability  that  the  block  is  round  given  that  it  is  from  the  second  bag    =  3\n                                                                                                       5\nP (R/3)=   Probability  that  the  block  is  round  given  that  it  is  from  the  third  bag  =  5  =  1\n                                                                                                    5"},{"type":"p","text":"P (R) = P (R/1) ∗ P (1) + P (R/2) ∗ P (2) + P (R/3) ∗ P (3) P (R) = (2/5) ∗ (1/3) + (3/5) ∗ (1/3) + 1 ∗ (1/3) = 2/3"}],"remember":["Conditional Probability: In this question, conditional probability is used to calculate the probability of choosing a round block given the bag from which it is chosen.","Total Probability Rule: The total probability rule is applied to find the overall probability of choosing a round block by considering the probabilities from each bag.","Probability of an Event: Probability is a measure of the likelihood that an event will occur. It ranges from 0 to 1, where 0 indicates impossibility and 1 indicates certainty.","Random Experiment: A random experiment is a process or course of action whose outcome cannot be predicted with certainty."]},{"id":"3619","page":43,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"1/5"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"3/10"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"1/3"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"2/5"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"There are three different bags. The first bag contains 3 square blocks and 2 round blocks. The second bag contains 2 square blocks and 3 round blocks. The third bag contains 5 round blocks. In an experiment, a bag is randomly chosen and then a block picked. Given a round block was selected, what is the probability it came from the second bag?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Let \"1\" be the event that the first bag is picked, \"2\" be the event that the second bag is picked, \"3\" be the event that the third bag is picked, and \"R\" be the event that a round block is drawn P (2/R) = P (R/2) ∗ P (2)/P (R) P (R) = P (R/1) ∗ P (1) + P (R/2) ∗ P (2) + P (R/3) ∗ P (3) P (R) = (2/5) ∗ (1/3) + (3/5) ∗ (1/3) + 1 ∗ (1/3) = 2/3 P (2/R) = (3/5) ∗ (1/3)/(2/3) = 3/10"}],"remember":["Conditional Probability: This concept is used to calculate the probability of an event given that another event has already occurred.","Bayes' Theorem: A mathematical formula used to determine the conditional probability of an event based on prior knowledge of conditions that might be related to the event.","Probability of an Event: The likelihood of an event occurring, usually represented as a number between 0 and 1.","Random Experiment: A process that leads to one of several possible outcomes with uncertain results."]},{"id":"3620","page":43,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"40%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"50%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"51%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"53%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"There are two bags with red and white balls. The first bag has 5 red and 5 white balls."},{"type":"p","text":"The second bag has 3 red and 2 white balls. A bag is randomly selected, and a ball is drawn without replacement. If the ball is red, another ball is selected from the same bag. If the ball is white, another ball is selected from the other bag. What is the probability that the second ball drawn is red?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"P (2nd red) = P (2nd red/1st red) ∗ P (1st red) + P (2nd red/1st white) ∗ P (1st white)"},{"type":"p","text":"P (1st red) = 1 ∗ 1/2 + 1/2 ∗ 3/5 = .55"},{"type":"pre","text":"2"},{"type":"p","text":"P (2nd red/1st red) = (1/2) ∗ (4/9) + (3/5) ∗ (2/4) = .52"},{"type":"p","text":"P (2nd red/1st white) = (1/2) ∗ (3/5) + (2/5) ∗ (1/2) = .5"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (1st  white) =   1  ∗ 5/10 + 1/2  ∗ 2/5 =  .45\n                   2"},{"type":"p","text":"P (2nd red) = .55 ∗ .52 + .45 ∗ .50 = .51 or 51%"},{"type":"p","text":"Alternative approach using a probability tree"}],"remember":["Conditional probability is the likelihood of an event occurring given that another event has already occurred.","Bayes' Theorem is a formula that describes how to update the probabilities of hypotheses when given evidence.","Total probability rule states that the sum of the probabilities of all possible outcomes in a sample space is equal to 1.","Independence of events means that the occurrence of one event does not affect the probability of the occurrence of another event.","Random variables are variables whose values depend on outcomes of a random phenomenon.","Expected value is the long-run average value of repetitions of the experiment it represents.","Probability tree diagrams are graphical representations of possible outcomes and their probabilities in sequential events."]},{"id":"3621","page":45,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"1/10"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"1/5"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"2/5"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"1/3"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"In a game, a coin is flipped. If the coin is heads, the player rolls one die. If the coin turns up tails, the player rolls two dice and the player moves their playing piece the number of spots shown on the die or dice. Given that on a player's turn, he moves 5 spaces, what is the probability he flipped tails on the coin?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"P (5) = P (5/T) ∗ P (T ) + P (5/H ) ∗ P (H ) P (5) = (1/9) ∗ (1/2) + (1/6) ∗ (1/2) = 5/36"},{"type":"p","text":"P (T /5) = P (5/T ) ∗ P (T )/P (5)"},{"type":"p","text":"P (T /5) = (1/9) ∗ (1/2)/(5/36) = 2/5"},{"type":"p","text":"Further explanation: The probability P(5/H) is a conditional probability, indicating that the player has already flipped the coin and obtained a head (H). Given this information, the player only rolls the dice once, as the coin is showing a head. It's important to note that rolling a dice has six possible outcomes (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6), each with an equal probability of 1/6."},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore, the probability of the player rolling the dice and obtaining a 5 is 1/6."},{"type":"p","text":"The question states that if the coin turns up tails, the player will roll two dice. In this case, the player will have a sample size of 36 (6 outcomes for the first dice multiplied by 6 outcomes for the second dice). The question further specifies that the player moves 5 times. If the coin turns out to be tails, the possible outcomes are (1,4), (2,3), (3,2), and (4,1) out of the total sample space of 36 outcomes. Therefore, the probability P(5|T) is calculated as 4/36, which simplifies to 1/9."}],"remember":["Conditional probability is the likelihood of an event occurring given that another event has already occurred.","Sample space is the set of all possible outcomes in a probability experiment.","Probability of an event is calculated as the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes in the sample space.","Independence of events means that the occurrence of one event does not affect the probability of another event occurring.","Bayes' Theorem is a formula that describes how to update the probability of a hypothesis as more evidence or information becomes available."]},{"id":"3624","page":47,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"21%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"33%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"43%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"58%"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An insurance company writes business in three territories: A, B and C. They have 150 policyholders in territory A, 250 in territory B and 300 in territory C. A person is twice as likely to have a claim in territory B than territory A and 3 times as likely to have a claim in territory C than territory A. On average, 50 people have a claim every policy period. Given a claim occurs, what is the probability it was a policyholder in territory C?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"          P(C|Claim)    P(Claim|C) ∗  P(C)\n                      = P(Claim)\nP(Claim) = P(Claim|A) ∗ P(A) + P(Claim|B) ∗ P(B) + P(Claim|C) ∗ P(C)\n              = x ∗ ( 170500) + 2x ∗ ( 275000) + 3x ∗ ( 370000)\n              = x ∗ ( 1750500)"},{"type":"p","text":"But we also know that the probability of a claim from all territories combined is 50/700. Thus,"},{"type":"pre","text":"1550x     50\n 700   =  700  ⇒  x  =  0.032\nBut,   P(C)  =  300\n                700"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore,"},{"type":"pre","text":"P(C|Claim) =  3 ∗ 0.032 ∗    300\n                             700\n                         50\n                            700\n            = 0.576 ≈ 58%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3625","page":47,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.275"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.15"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.245"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.95"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A test for heart disease results in a correct positive diagnosis 95% of the time and a correct negative diagnosis 99% of the time. 25% of the population has heart disease. What is the probability of a positive test?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Let's define the following events: + = positive diagnosis - = negative diagnosis H = heart disease H' = no heart disease P (+|H) = .95 P (−|H) = .99 P (+) = P (+|H) ∗ P (H ) + P (+|H ′) ∗ P (H ′)"},{"type":"pre","text":"= .95 ∗ .25 + .01 ∗ .75 = .245"},{"type":"p","text":"Alternative Approach"},{"type":"p","text":"The question asks for the probability of a positive test result, whether or not the person being tested actually has heart disease. This can be obtained using the total probability rule, taking into account both the probability of a true positive result (the person has heart disease and the test is positive) and a false positive result (the person does not have heart disease but the test is positive)."},{"type":"p","text":"The given data are:"},{"type":"pre","text":"The prevalence of heart disease in the population (P(D)), which is 25%, or 0.25.\nThe probability of a correct positive diagnosis (P(T|D)), which is 95%, or 0.95.\nThe probability of a correct negative diagnosis (P(~T|~D)), which is 99%, or 0.99."},{"type":"p","text":"The probability of not having the disease (P(~D)) is 1 - P(D), or 0.75. The probability of a positive test given that the person does not have the disease (P(T|~D)) is 1 - P(~T|~D), or 0.01"},{"type":"p","text":"Now, using the total probability rule, we can calculate the total probability of a positive test result (P(T)), whether the person being tested has the disease or not:"},{"type":"p","text":"P(T) = P(D) × P(T|D) + P(~D) × P(T|~D) Substituting the known values: P(T) = 0.25 × 0.95 + 0.75 × 0.01 = 0.2375 + 0.0075 = 0.245 So, the probability of a positive test result, whether the person has heart disease or not, is 0.245 (or 24.5% when expressed as a percentage)"}],"remember":["Prevalence rate of a disease in a population affects the interpretation of diagnostic test results.","True positive rate (Sensitivity) and true negative rate (Specificity) are important measures of a diagnostic test's accuracy.","False positive rate and false negative rate are also crucial in evaluating the performance of a diagnostic test.","Total Probability Rule is a fundamental concept in probability theory used to calculate the overall probability of an event by considering all possible outcomes.","Bayes' Theorem is another important concept in probability theory that describes the probability of an event based on prior knowledge of conditions that might be related to the event."]},{"id":"3643","page":50,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"2 5"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"7 10"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"4 5"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"9 10"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A company insures red and black cars, male and female drivers and writes policies in 2 territories A and B. There are 300 male drivers and 200 female drivers in total. There are 150 males who drive red cars and 100 females who drive red cars. 100 male and 100 female drivers live in territory A and 50 of each, males and females, drive red cars in territory A. What is the probability that a randomly selected driver is either female or lives in territory B?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Using:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (A ∪ B) = P (A) + P (B) − P (A ∩ B)"},{"type":"p","text":"We need,"},{"type":"p","text":"P (female or living in territory B) = P (Female) + P ( Living in territory B) − P (female and living in territory B"},{"type":"pre","text":"=  200  +  300  −  100  =  400  =  4\n   500     500     500     500     5"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"5302","page":50,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"91%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"94%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"87%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"89%"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"In a financial institution following the Three Lines of Defense Model, the following information is given:"},{"type":"pre","text":"The First Line of Defense (Business Units) detects 70% of all operational risk incidents.\nThe Second Line of Defense (Risk Management) detects 60% of the remaining\nundetected incidents.\nThe Third Line of Defense (Internal Audit) detects 50% of the remaining undetected\nincidents after the Second Line of Defense."},{"type":"p","text":"What is the probability that an operational risk incident is detected by at least one of the three lines of defense?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"To calculate the overall probability of an operational risk incident being detected, we can use the formula for the probability of the complement event: P (A ∪ B ∪ C) = 1 − P (A′ ∩ B′ ∩ C ′) Where A, B, and C represent the detection probabilities of the three lines of defense, and A', B', and C' represent their complements (i.e., the probabilities of not detecting an incident). First, we need to find the probabilities of not detecting an incident for each line of defense:"},{"type":"pre","text":"A′ = 1 − A = 1 − 0.7 = 0.3\nB′ = 1 − B = 1 − 0.6 = 0.4\nC ′ = 1 − C = 1 − 0.5 = 0.5"},{"type":"p","text":"Next, we need to find the probability of none of the lines of defense detecting an incident, which is given by the product of their respective probabilities of not detecting an incident: P (A′ ∩ B′ ∩ C ′) = A′ × B′ × C ′ = 0.3 × 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.06 Now we can find the probability of at least one line of defense detecting an incident: P (A ∪ B ∪ C) = 1 − P (A′ ∩ B′ ∩ C ′) = 1 − 0.06 = 0.94"}],"remember":["The Three Lines of Defense Model is a risk management framework used in the financial industry to ensure robust risk management practices.","The First Line of Defense consists of business units that own and manage risks on a day-to-day basis.","The Second Line of Defense involves risk management functions that oversee and monitor the risks taken by the business units.","The Third Line of Defense comprises internal audit functions that provide independent assurance on the effectiveness of risk management processes.","Probability calculations often involve understanding complements and using them to find the probability of the desired event.","The concept of detecting operational risk incidents by multiple lines of defense helps in creating a comprehensive risk management framework."]},{"id":"5325","page":52,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"16.13%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"31.91%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"79.79%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"58.51%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"In a financial institution, the risk management department is monitoring the probability of a cyber-attack on their systems. Based on historical data, they have determined that the probability of a cyber-attack occurring in any given year is 5% (P(A)). They have also learned that a specific type of security breach (B) has been observed in 75% of past cyber-attacks (P (B|A) ). Additionally, the probability of observing this specific type of security breach without a cyber-attack is 1% (P (B|A′)). Using Bayes' theorem, what is the probability that a cyber-attack is occurring given that the specific type of security breach has been observed (P (A|B))?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"To solve this problem, we can use Bayes' theorem, which states:"},{"type":"p","text":"P (A|B) = P (B|A) × P (A)"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (B)"},{"type":"p","text":"We are given P (B|A), P (A), and P (B|A′), but we need to find P (B). We can do that using the Law of Total Probability:"},{"type":"p","text":"P (B) = P (B|A) × P (A) + P(B|A′) × P (A′)"},{"type":"p","text":"We know that P (A) = 0.05 and P (A′) = 1 − P (A) = 0.95. So, P (B) = 0.75 × 0.05 + 0.01 × 0.95 = 0.037 + 0.0095 = 0.04700."},{"type":"p","text":"Now we can apply Bayes' theorem:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P (A|B)  =  P (B|A) × P(A)        =  00.7.054×7000.05≈  0.79787\n                  P (B)"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus, the probability that a cyber-attack is occurring given that the specific type of security breach has been observed is approximately 79.79%."}],"remember":["Bayes' Theorem is a fundamental concept in probability theory that describes the probability of an event, based on prior knowledge of conditions that might be related to the event.","The Law of Total Probability is a fundamental rule relating marginal probabilities to conditional probabilities.","Conditional probability is the probability of an event given that another event has already occurred.","Understanding the relationship between conditional probabilities and marginal probabilities is crucial in risk management and decision-making processes."]}]);
